"In 2009 Punjab made it punishable to transplant paddy before a date in June notified by the state. The aim was to save groundwater. Farmers who planted in May, at the height of summer, had to keep their fields flooded while evaporation was fiercest, and the state's water table had been falling for decades as tube wells, many of them run on free electricity, drew more from the aquifers than the rains put back. Moving transplantation closer to the arrival of the monsoon would let the rain do some of the work that the pumps had been doing.",
"By its own measure the law worked: studies of the following years found that the water table fell more slowly where it was enforced. But the law moved the end of the season as well as its start. Paddy transplanted later is harvested later, and in Punjab the paddy harvest is followed within weeks by the sowing of wheat. A later harvest left farmers less time to clear their fields, and the cheapest way to clear a field of paddy straw in a hurry is to set fire to it. Satellite records show the burning shifting later into the autumn, and studies have linked the shift to the smoke that settles over Delhi each November.",
"It would be easy to conclude that the law simply traded one harm for another. That conclusion is too quick. The smoke is not the inevitable price of saving water; it is the price of saving water while leaving everything else as it was: the short gap before wheat, the support prices that make paddy the safest crop, the free power that makes pumping costless at the margin. A farmer who could sow wheat directly into the stubble, with a machine that cuts and spreads the straw as it goes, would have no reason to burn, whatever date he planted his paddy. Such machines exist; they are costly, and many smallholders cannot get one on the days when their fields are ready.",
